A||||71||MooCow||3.4 or 3.8 or V8||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||08-16-2000||07:47 PM||||Okay I want Everyone to vote on this.  What engine should I get next summer and what do you think would be the best as far as long term investment(not having to worry about it once it is in).  the 3.4 DOHC,  The 3.8 naturally aspired or the 3.8 Supercharged,  or the V8 (which the v8 is gonna cost me an arm and a leg for the swap)  I plan on having between 3,000 and 4,000 when April rolles around. I may have more but I am going to college so can't work to much.  Which of those three engines would look run and perform the best and the easiest to put in.  I may do the swaps by myself but I am not sure yet.<P>                               Thanks Ken||||reg||5
Z||000001||whamel||08-16-2000||09:09 PM||||I'm curious as to why you're saying the V8 is going to cost you an arm and a leg for the swap?  I am planning on going with the V-8 mainly because it is a bit less expensive than the last time I checked the V-6 kits.  Maybe I missed something and I'd like to get that straight before I start!<BR>By the way, since you're in Muskegon, when I start on my swap in the Fall/winter when I get back (I really hope that's the plan!), I'll try to remember to drop you a line.  It's not that far if you want to stop by and check it out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||08-16-2000||09:48 PM||||Unless you do all the work yourself $3-4000 maybe will put in a new 2.8.<P>The 3.8 s/c is probably the best bang for the buck.  The engine is very reliable, but you need the transmission if you go with an automatic, you need the ecm, wiring harness and probably lots of other stuff.  Unless the engine comes from the 3.8 gods (falls from the sky and is cheap), I'd be expecting $5-6000 for that type of conversion from someone like ACE or Archie or Ed Parks, Bob Steiger, Chris.  When you over estimate the cost, you won't be disappointed.  Everything costs more than you expect and takes longer.||||reg||1
Z||000003||whamel||08-16-2000||10:39 PM||||I'm not sure I follow you on this one, Old Lar.  I did a quick search for prices on the 3.8 SC engine and came up with about $700 for a 65k mile example without the Supercharger and $1500-$2500 for an undisclosed mile model with a supercharger.   (I did find them cheaper, but they were not listed as tested, running, or working.)  This would be most likely the '97 GP engine rated at 240HP - no warranty.  I found a few 350 V-8's at 350 HP for about $1599, crate motor 0 miles with a warranty.  Now, I admit - this was a quick search...I could probably do better on pricing.<P>I also re-browsed the kit prices, they are pretty much the same as far as I could tell.  Archie's master kit is pretty much comparable to the ACE kit with options.  I do have to say though, I liked Archie's attitude a LOT better than the Ace guy's when I was looking for kits.  I felt a little insulted by their (Ace's) answers to my questions, but hey - if I'm going to drop $3,000 on an engine kit I'd rather it's somebody who isn't going to trivialize my questions.  Archie responded quickly and gave me facts, not biased opinions.  (Thanks Archie!)<P>The only thing I can really see is gas economy - but if I was looking for that...well, I'd keep my Iron Duck, right?<P>I hope you don't get me wrong here - I'm going to do a swap as soon as my contract ends and I don't have to drive all over the freekin country every week and I'd really like to avoid making a big mistake while I still can.  I'm sure I'd be just as happy with my 350 mill as if I had put a 3.8 in there, but maybe I'm missing something important here and I'd like to clear that up before I go do something REAL stupid. (again!)<BR>Thanks,<BR>Wade<p>[This message has been edited by whamel (edited 08-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||MooCow||08-16-2000||11:29 PM||||Well I also liked Arcjie alot better than ACE.  The Archie told me that it was gonna cost me about $7,100 for a V8 swap with him doing it and without a V8 still.  It was also with the master kit.  Now if I buy the Master kit or deluxe kit will this allow me to easily swap my v6 to a V8 without any problems right??  I really don't know to much on this swap that's why I was going to have someone else do it.  But there is no way I'm going to spend $7,100 without even a V8.  My friend is going to help me now do any swap I wish cause he knows a lot more than I do on this subject so I may still go V8.  Hey whamel e-mail me so we can chat.<P>                             Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000005||Archie||08-17-2000||01:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MooCow:<BR><B> Archie told me that it was gonna cost me about $7,100 for a V8 swap with him doing it and without a V8 still.<BR>                             Thanks Ken</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well let's see what that $7100 consists of, $2950 for the Automatic Master-Build kit, $1150 for the JPT modified transmission, $200 for the transmission core, that leaves $2800 for misc. parts & our labor. (our labor rate figures out to a lot less than the $70/hour labor rate in this area)<P>Just as whamel pointed out(for a used V-6), I'm sure that you can get a used Chevy V-8 65,000 mile engine for $700 in just about any junkyard in any town.<P>IMHO, if someone is going to pay me $2800 labor to do a good swap of a V-8 into their Fiero, then they would be making a mistake using an engine that had 65K on it already.<P>If your goal is to spend $3 to $4K, a V-8 could be done but you would need to do your own labor.<P>I would also point out that when comparing engine swaps, you need to compare apples to apples. If you are going to use new engine prices for the SBC then use new engine prices for the 3.4 & 3.8 engines. Consider that it may not be fair to compare a "used" V-6 swap cost to a "new" V-8 swap cost.<P>I hope this has been of some help.<P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||RobertVol||08-17-2000||01:13 AM||||Sorry but to buck the trend I have to say 3.8 SC. But I was lucky I covered most of the cost with a steal on a virtually new engine. The 3.8 if found can be used in the conversion that is done by someone else. I did use ACE and for what I am hearing from most must have been one of those lucky customers. I paid them for the complete conversion plus had them put in a centerforce II clutch while doing that for about $3900.00 dollars. remember I already had the engine though. Got it from a GM mechanic at a local Buick dealership. it was a steal. but I get lower gas mileage and it is a VERY reliable engine. I have NO PROBLEMS other than GEAR assignment (thanks Terryk) which fixed locally and with SUPPORT from ACE. So again I must have been lucky with them but got REALLY good service. If you can find a good deal on the engine I would prefer the 3.8SC but I am partial <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>JMHO <BR><P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT Top 3.8 SC t top <BR>88GT T top 55k for sale<BR>  <A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A>  <BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fortunecity.com/marina/strand/1086/501b4920.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Big O||08-17-2000||01:35 AM||||I gotta tell you I love my 3.8 SC.  It is quick, reliable and very economical.  I had ACE do the complete installation and he charged me $8,000.00.  It only took another $2,000 to fix his mistakes, but other than that and the fact thet the "0" mile power plant he said he put in failed the tranny after about 3,000 miles, he did a "good" job.  I had Chris West straighten out some of the problems, and some I took care of myself.  Even with all of the grief I've had with the swap I would still pick the 3.8 SC over the V8 because I think it will last a lot longer and not be as hard on the rest of the car.  I now have approx 15,000 miles on the installation and its running great.  I get around 35 mpg at 80 mph (2000 rpm) cruising.  Besides, I would probably just burn the tires up on the V8 and wind up with a pack of tickets.  ||||reg||14
Z||000008||Monkeyman||08-17-2000||01:50 AM||||Rebuilt 2.8--less than $2000<BR>3.4 (&gt;30k miles from Ed Parks)--$2500<BR>3.6 (brand new--well, reconditioned with a warranty from Ed Parks)--$3300<BR>(All of the above include labor.)<BR>3.8 S/C--around $8000 (BENGAL4 is getting one done now)<BR>V8--???  Ask Archie.  He's the expert.<P>I'd say the V8 has the most potential for raw power, but I plan on getting a 3.6.  Most bang for the buck, IMHO.  The way I understand it, the 3.6 is just a 3.4 overbored.  Plenty of go fast stuff for that.  Besides, no one but you and your mechanic will know it's anything other than stock until you stomp on the loud pedal.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||08-17-2000||02:26 AM||||Just a thought about reliability. But I know for a fact that most Taxi cab company's and police departments use the SBC V-8's and they get many miles out of them.<BR>A relative works for a major city police department and they have SBC's that have gone 300,000 miles and are still running strong. The SBC has been around for what roughly 30 some odd years. Probably will last another 30! <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||08-17-2000||07:30 AM||||I also have an ACE SC3800 5sp conversion and so far 34K trouble-free miles. A 3.4 conversion by Parks can't be beat for value,Monkey's figures seem about right.||||reg||1
Z||000011||whamel||08-17-2000||08:19 AM||||I'm still a little confused I guess - how is an engine that has less power and costs more out of the box a better value than an engine that already has more power and costs less?  Forgive me here guys - I'm a little slow I guess...<BR>I have a friend who has one of those hopped up brand new GTX or GTP Grand Prixs.  He's basically put the entire catalog of the grandprixstore.com in the dang thing and my '92 Daytona would still blow it's doors off (as long as it was a rolling race and not off the line, mind you).  My goal isn't to spend $10,000 so I can have a car that my old Daytona can beat.<BR>From the positive comments I hear about both, they're a good swap either way and it's just personal preference, right?  I mean how the heck much more power do you need out of a streetable Fiero?  (I can't believe I just wrote that...)  I figure 240-350 HP should be more than enough to move that car a lot faster than it should with either engine!  I've tried to factor in weight, ease of maintenance, overall cost, etc. etc. and I just don't see this 'more bang for your buck' that I keep seeing about the V-6 swap.<BR>On another note - what's the oil change interval on the 3.8SC?  Is it 1500 miles like most wound up engines or is it standard?  That was the big drawback to my Daytona was absolutely having to change the oil every 1500 miles, having to use only synthetic, only using premium unleaded fuel....yuck - it was a maintenance nightmare. (and the last intercooled turbo I'll buy!)||||reg||1
Z||000012||whamel||08-17-2000||08:22 AM||||Oh yeah, and one more thing I was thinking about - before you even THINK about doing an engine swap of any sort you had better call your local DMV or whatever and make sure you can.  I had an old '81 Camaro that had a 305 in it - I did the 305-&gt;350 swap and found out that since I had installed a different engine in my car than was production, I could no longer register my car in the state of Ohio.  Now keep in mind, this was quite a while ago and it was in Cincinnati - but I ended up having to register the car in Kentucky to get around it.  I'd rather find out in advance that I can't do the swap rather than after I've done it again!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Philphine||08-17-2000||09:43 AM||||some thing 've been wondering but haven't looked into since i'm no where near ready to make a swap. <P>with the new aluminum chevy small block available and also aluminum northstar engines that are being used even in old hot rods and stuff, i wonder if older aluminum small blocks are less expensive since aluminum engines are not quite as rare now.<P>just from a "dreaming" stand point, thats the engine i'd like to have.  <P><P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000014||RobertVol||08-17-2000||09:49 AM||||Grand prix = what about 3300 lbs?<BR>Fiero = approx 2700 - 2800<P>Your PTW ratio is a dramatic difference.<P>I dont know about Phil and what yr his 3.8SC is but mine is GENII 99 engine and like Phil have the 5 speed, and just Finally took it out for HARD run amd we got 13.6 -13.8 1/4 times. not to shabby. Those are some pretty good numbers for the v-6<P>also I dont have that much more upkeep. I run 93 Octane as a preference and change my oil at 5000 miles..again a preference. I think you could be more loose on that upkeep schedule but again this is my preference. it could go longer than that no problem. <P>Most GM mechanics will tell you the 3800 engine is one of the most well built and reliable engines in the world. I think your paying here for the technology of 2 less cylinders for CLOSE to the same power and save alot in GAS. in the long run a better decision. also the 3800 or 3.8 design has been around for long time as well. maybe not quite as long as the SBC 350 but it has been around a while||||reg||1
Z||000015||StuGood||08-17-2000||09:52 AM||||This is one of the more interesting threads I've read here.  Sorry I can't offer any personal opinions on engine swaps, because I haven't done one (yet).  Thanks, MooCow, for starting this topic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !<P>The only pieces of information missing so far are - what sort of fuel mileage do you V8 guys get?  What type of fuel do you run? If it gives 35mpg, but you have to run premium gas, that's no good for me.<P>Ease of maintenance is an important consideration, too.  Anyone care to share their experiences?     <P>Please don't tell me that if I wanted fuel economy, I'd run iron dukes.  It's not an all-or-nothing thing, as we all know.  "Do I want to go fast, or do I want good fuel economy?"  I'm not picky - I want <B>both</B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !<P>Seems to me, so far, that the 3800 offers significantly more power than the 2.8's, better mileage, is quite reliable, and uses fairly common engine parts.  Timing chains eventually let go, though, and it's not a free-running ("clearance") engine.  Can you put in a steel sprocket, and pretty much forget about that problem? <P><BR>        ||||reg||10
Z||000016||RobertVol||08-17-2000||09:53 AM||||BTW also ...the $8000 price tag for the conversion seems a little high. unless they had more done to the car while in for the conversion. Mine was as I said a FULL conversion at ACE and it was maybe total $4000.00 maybe a little more. Not counting the engine. Is that the other cost you guys are adding in? that seems a little pricey butt you never know ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Phil||08-17-2000||10:25 AM||||Robert<BR>Mine is a seriesII also (97 Buick). Had a chance to get it on a dyno and it gave 222HP/273ft-lb(wheels). That's right up there with SBC and none of the handling probs. One word of advise with any hp upgrade save some pennys for new shocks and BRAKES||||reg||1
Z||000018||elfiero||08-17-2000||10:39 AM||||Here is my .02cents.<BR>I've done two seriesI3800 swaps,<BR>one SBC swap,<BR>I'm working on a seriesIIS/C3800 swap<BR>The best bang for the buck is a regular seriesI3800! this has the least muss&fuss,<BR>it is by far the cheapest,and won't scare<BR>the crap out of most mechanics if you need<BR>to get somthing done.<BR>Here is MY price list to work on customer<BR>cars:<BR>  1. 3800 swap w/auto trans.    2,500.00<BR>  2.S/C 3800 swap w/auto trans. 3,500.00<BR>  3.SBC TPI swap w/auto trans.  5,500.00<BR>  4.manual trans. ADD           1,000.00<BR>  With all above prices,you supply the<BR>  engine.I have reliable sources for good<BR>  used engines at a reasonable price if<BR>  you like.it is your responsibility to<BR>  transport the car to minneapolis,but I'll<BR>  be happy to deliver it when its finished.<BR>  I know there are people out there who will<BR>  do it cheaper,But none who are better. <BR>  Somthing to think about: no matter what<BR>  engine you put in,when its finished,it <BR>  should look like GM put it in,not the local<BR>  greasemonkeys!Just somthing to consider...<BR>  ||||reg||10
Z||000019||batboy||08-17-2000||11:50 AM||||Lots of good points in this thread. I'd like to add a couple of comments too, remember I own a 350 V8 Fiero. First off, you probably can't go wrong with any of the 3.4, 3800 or small block Chevy engine swaps. The 3.4 is probably going to be the cheapest. The 3800 and 350 V8 swaps will probably be fairly comparable in price.<P>But, there is more to look at then expense. I'm assuming since you want to do a swap, you're looking for more power. Do you want massive straight-line acceleration or are you more interested in how the car handles the curves?<P>I'd reread Phil's last post. He's right about the 3800 s/c dyno numbers being close to a stock small block Chevy numbers. The beauty of the 3800 swap is that it fits much better in the engine compartment and is lighter.<P>The 3800 is a damn reliable engine, but so is the 350 V8. Speaking from experience, somethings on the 350 are very hard to work on in a Fiero. I don't know how I'd ever get the firewall valve cover off without removing the deck hinge and maybe some other stuff.<P>I have a carbed 350, so I'm sure the FI 350's get better gas mileage, but I only get about 18 mpg. Of course I can't stay off the throttle, gotta hear those twice pipes growling. The 3.4 and 3800 will be the gas mileage champs.<P>Some of you guys are still only looking at horsepower numbers when you should be looking at torque figures too. It's been said many times here: horsepower sells cars, but torque wins races. The exception would be oval track racing where you need high reving horsepower.<P>I love my 350 powered Fiero, but I've had to do lots of suspension upgrades in order to get the handling back to where I think it should be. I also have small block Chevy engines and parts up the ying-yang. However, I probably wouldn't of done a complete V8 Fiero swap myself from scratch (I bought the Batmobile as an unfinished project with the engine already installed).<P>I have a 87 SE with a dead motor. I'm seriously considering the 3.4 swap in it (not until next year at the soonest). If you build it with forged pistons, you can always add nitrous later if you want more power at the stoplight grand prix.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Megafiero||08-17-2000||12:07 PM||||Hey Monkeyman,  are you sure about that 3.6 being a overbored 3.4?  A 3.4L is actually only 204.4 CI (not the 209 it is stated at), and that is acutally a 3.3 in all realitly, so even if you overbored that motor .060" over  it is still just a 3.4L.  Are you sure that the 3.6 is not a buick motor?  Sorry if I'm sounding like a know-it-all, but I wanted to clear that up, and I'v heard talk before on 3.6's and am wondering what they really are.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tir.com/~jcabic/megasig.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Megafiero (edited 08-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||MooCow||08-17-2000||12:09 PM||||I am not really concerned with gas that much.  I just want to be able to punch and be able to roast my tires in 1st, 2nd, and possibly 3rd like my friends 5 speed mustang. I just want to be ablt to go faster than a Camaro or Mustang.  Just hit the gas and get pushed back in my seat.<BR>    Does the Fiero handle way different when if I put a V8 or even the 3.8 in it?  I also need an engine that is realiable and mostly maintenance free.  The 3.4 swap I know I can do by myself but the others to me seem difficult so I would have comone else do it but will not have that money next year when I want the new engine.  Keep the posts comming.<BR>                              Thanks Ken ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Monkeyman||08-17-2000||01:18 PM||||Megafiero--The info I got on the 3.4/3.5/3.6 was from Ed Parks (Fiero Factory).  I don't know anything about motors personally.  He said that he could get 3.5s and 3.6s from one of the reman places (like Jasper, but I think he used someone else).  My memory could be a little fuzzy, but I thought he said that the 3.5/3.6 was an overbored 3.4.  I know he said that it was the same installation as the 3.4 and it would look just as stock as the 3.4.  I might give him another call soon and verify my info.  Seems to me that the 3.4 is different than the 3.3.  Don't they make both??  I remember that the 3.4 is the Twin Dual Double Overhead Cam thing.  (something like that)||||reg||1
Z||000023||Formula 18||08-17-2000||02:30 PM||||This is where the Whole 3.4L swap gets a little fuzzy. MonkeyMan's right there is a 3.4TDC (twin dual cam) and then there is a 3.4L pushrod out of 93-95 Camaro's. The pushrod engines swap in without any problems and you can even dress 'em up to look just like the stock 2.8L (valve cover, penlum, ect). The other is a high-tech high reving engine but requires a computer swap and some other stuff to make it spin. for more info look here <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/ca2/34Performance/dohc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/ca2/34Performance/dohc.html</A> <P>The problem is that none of the pieces of the 3.4TDC will work with the 3.4L pushrod. I think there is even a 3.4L with a roller cam too.<BR>Right?  <P>------------------<BR>Brian<BR> -88 Formula<p>[This message has been edited by Formula 18 (edited 08-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||Megafiero||08-17-2000||02:39 PM||||I think GM did make a 3.3 for some buick cars in the erliy 90's or so, but any one of the 3.4L chevy engines are actually not true 3.4's if you do the math they calculate out to 3.3, but GM rounds the number up to make it sound bigger.<P>They way you can do this is as follows.<P><BR>Roughly take "PIE" (3.14) multiplied by (half the cylinder dia. squared) multiplied by the (stroke). Then you multiplie it by the number of cylinders.  that equals cubic inches, to get litres, dived the CI by 62.01.<P>It takes 62.01 CI to = 1 L<P>ex. of a 3.4L<P>3.62" cylinder diamiter divided by 2 =1.81<BR>1.81 squared = 3.2761 (multipliy that by the storke "3.31") = 10.843891 (multipliy that by PIE "3.14" = 34.04981774 (multipliy by cylinder number "6" = 204.2989064 or simple 204 cubic invhes.<P>204.2989064 divided by 62.01 = 3.294612263 or 3.3 litres.<P>sorry if that is confusing, I tryed to keep it simple.  IF you question this  you can try it with any engine.  Try a chevy 350,<BR>bore= 4" stroke= 3.48, and it does work.<P>Oh ya they way you can tell if overbore will increase this is by just adding it to the cyliner bore.  EX. 3.4 = 3.62 + .060" overbore = 3.68"<P>Hope that helps you out some, and if it makes you confused, I can try to explane it a little different for ya.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.tir.com/~jcabic/megasig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000025||pollock||08-17-2000||03:20 PM||||First off, thanks to everyone for keeping their cool and making this a good discussion topic.<P>Second, I have not done a Fiero engine swap.  So what i have to add is just my own observation.  I have done a 6 to 8 Mustang swap, and various v8 swaps in other cars.<P>I think the 3.4 is far cheaper, but offers far less power.  Nitrous would help even the score, and still be cheaper than the other two options.<BR>I think that the 3.8SC and SBC will be comparable in performance.<BR>An SBC that is a lot more powerful than a 3.8SC would be too much for a Fiero drive line.<BR>3.8SC will probably get through emissions in most states; it's probably cleaner than any factory 2.8s.  I know the gas mileage #s are better than mine.<BR>SBC carbed would be easier to install than a 3.8, from an electronics standpoint.<BR>I wouldn't go with a naturally aspirated 3.8.... there are so many SC ones running around, and there seems to be very little difference in the installation.<BR>3.8 would fit better, i think it would be easier to work on.<BR>FI SBC could probably come close to the 3.8SC on mpg, but this would be the most difficult, expensive swap of the lot.<P>As for me, i'm a bit too scared of the electronics work on the 3.8SC, or i'd go for it.<P>I want to keep my fiero fuel injected, so the carbed sbc is out.<P>I'll probably go with the NOS 3.4.||||reg||1
Z||000026||whamel||08-17-2000||03:21 PM||||I agree - this is probably one of the most interesting threads I have read here!  It's exactly what I've been wondering all along.  I appreciate all the input I've seen here.  I'm still kinda fuzzy on quite a few points, but I do think I have a better understanding of some alternative viewpoints though.  After reading all I've seen so far though - I'll go with the V-8, carb (no EFI), simple setup - it's what I feel most comfortable with.  I am still quite a bit unsure about the newer v-6 and how it will relate to the Fiero.<BR>I've got a few advantages though - I'll be doing it myself, I don't care about economy (in fact, I'm counting on it being really bad!), I work on my own cars, and if it goes down - I have another Fiero or like any of 5 others cars I could drive.  I guess if it was going to be a daily driver and I had a commute, some of those economy issues might come into play.  It really seems to be based on your need, desire, or plan for the car.<BR>Two last questions though:<BR>1) What kind of maintenace schedule do the 3.8's follow?<BR>2) What's the average final power output of a 3.8SC after the swap?  I know what I can get out of a 350 reasonably cheap, but I never bothered with the smaller engines.  (I'm originally from Kentucky and grew up thinking big blocks were better most of my life.)<BR>3) Okay - three questions - do the 3.8's need to be run on Premium only or can they settle on say, midgrade gas?<BR>Thanks,<BR>Wade||||reg||1
Z||000027||Old Lar||08-17-2000||04:41 PM||||My 99 Buick GS 3.8 S/C gets oil change every 3000 miles.  I'm using synthetic 10-30.  The engine is rated at 240 HP at 5500 rpm. What it would be in a Fiero after a swap (?) It is supposed to get 92 octane.  The higher octane, I think, is for the oxygenated fuel for cleaner burning and passing the emission tests. I have put in 89 octane and have noticed no difference, then I'm not in a daily drag race with it.  I don't even think I've had it up to 5500 rpm's yet.||||reg||1
Z||000028||MooCow||08-17-2000||06:33 PM||||So whick one is easier to do by yourself the 3.8SC or a V8. I have an SE 4 speed so will that bolt right up to any engine?  If i go to an auto with the new engine will that be more difficult to swap or not as long as I get the tranny that came with the engine?<P><BR>                                 Thanks Ken<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000029||stimpy||08-17-2000||07:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RobertVol:<BR><B>BTW also ...the $8000 price tag for the conversion seems a little high. unless they had more done to the car while in for the conversion. Mine was as I said a FULL conversion at ACE and it was maybe total $4000.00 maybe a little more. Not counting the engine. Is that the other cost you guys are adding in? that seems a little pricey butt you never know </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I take it you are talking about Big O's swap...  Yep, he got the full catalog treatment. Poly bushings, Eibach springs, KYB shocks and struts, rear swaybar, and 16x7 Sendels and BFG Radial T/A 245/45s. Of course this prescribed suspension treatment shredded the right rear tire against the strut spring mounting plate on hard acceleration. DOH!<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000030||RobertVol||08-17-2000||07:40 PM||||If power on tap is what you want the 3800 sc will give it about the same as a reasonable SBC v-8. it is a lighter engine and the handling is better IMHO. <P>My maintenence schedule is close to Phils. I have on occasion ran 89oct gas with no problems either. The engine work on the 3.8. Most of the work can be done by a reasonable mechanic I would think. But then again I couldnt really say because mine has never been in for problems  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> well other than gear assignment problem which was easily fixed. but other than that NOOOOOO problems<P>and yeah Phil I forgot to include suspensions mods <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> that would need to be added into the cost but the stock suspension can handle it but with all that speed why would you want to do that ? ||||reg||1
Z||000031||MooCow||08-17-2000||09:36 PM||||If the 3.8 SB isn't that difficult then I would go with but actually how tuff is it?<BR>Can i get it done in a month if I work on it all the time in the summer.  Or is this thing going to be so hard that i will be spending all this money and have it not be right?  Doesn't a kit supply everything I need to get the job done??<P>                           Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000032||batboy||08-17-2000||11:11 PM||||The 3800 swap is easier if you use the donor car's auto overdrive. The drive axles have to be modified, but it's not a super big deal.<P>I don't remember who said they wanted to be able to smoke the tires in 2 or 3 gears, but if you do that with a tail heavy Fiero with a big engine too many times, it's going to cost you big money. Trust me.<P>If you've never done any kind of engine swap before, a Fiero swap might not the one you want to try first. Engine swaps always cost more money and are much more work than most people imagine. Most people I've heard talk about their Fiero swaps say that even with the complete kit, you'll still have lots more little things to buy, beg, borrow, or steal in order to finish the job.<P>One point that's been overlooked, if you're truly after brute power and massive torque, the only way to go is the SBC V8. The 3800 s/c might be it's equal if the 350 is stock, but there is sooo much more hotrodding you can do with the V8. However, even with my tired ol' stock 350 with lots of torque in my light little Batmobile, I've only lost one race (a C-4 Vette). I also beat that same Vette once too. I lost a roll and go at 70 MPH, but won from a standing start. Torque in a light car rules and horsepower drools.<P>The point is, Phil's 3800 Fiero is probably more powerful than my V8 right now. So how much more power do you need than that, unless this is just a playtoy or a race car?||||reg||1
Z||000033||Big O||08-18-2000||01:17 AM||||The $8,000 I quoted for the swap in my 86 GT was just the powerplant portion of it.  By the time I got through with the boys at ACE I'd dropped almost $12,000 for the swap, suspension mods, exhaust system, tires and wheels.   I get great miliage out of 89 octane gas and service every 3000 miles with standard 10/30 detergent oil.  I think I get a little better performance out of the higher octane gas when I run it, but not enough to justify the extra cost in my book. When doing this swap the chassis mods are next to nothing.  I don't think they needed to do anything to the drivelines.  The tough part is the electronics, and even ACE got it wrong (one wire out of place).  The electronics is the reason I shipped it off to Colorado to have the swap done there rather than finding someone in California to do the job.||||reg||1
Z||000034||Monkeyman||08-18-2000||02:36 AM||||I guess Ed Parks is talking about the pushrod version of the 3.4.  I don't know anything about the math, but GM calls it a 3.4 so that's what I'm calling it.  If I/you/we call it a 3.3, no one will know what I/you/we am/are referring to.||||reg||1
Z||000035||Dan86gt3.8||08-18-2000||02:43 AM||||Ohhh wow a flaming engine swap post  with 3.8 stuff and I am getting in on the tail end (booo).  I have a N/A 3.8 and it is nice but not that fast.  A 3.8 sc is a great engine to put in. Now that I have talked to some people you do not have to have a kit.  I went the mail order kit route and me and my dad did all of it ourselvs save for some welding and the exhaust system.  But then again when you get a kit you 1.) get the know how 2.)get tech support from someone that does this stuff all the time and 3.) get stuff like complete wiring harness, which I have to say was the nicest experience about the whole swap.  Just to be abale to plug it in and go.  But like I said earlier it can be done without a kit.  If I understand chris wests site right he can contract out the whole swap (ie couple hundred for the mounts, 300 for complete wiring harness, etc) someone correct me if I am wrong.  But then again a small block is also a good thought.  But like they said earlier they don't get as good of gas mileage, but then again they have ben around for 30+ years and is the staple of the hot rodding comunity.....<BR>But Someone mentioned drivetrain reliability The thing I mainly concerned about is the tranny.  I dont see how they could handle the torque of hopped up small blocks.  The fiero trannys are designed to handle what 150 lbs of torque?  Then you go put a v8 in that has twice that much.  I think that would be a disaster waiting to happen.  But then again I have never seen a group of guys that actually own v8 fieros get together and talk about v8 dirvetrains and how they hold up.  But there are many factirs like how built the engine is, if its hot-rooded, if its taken care of, if the tranny was "beefed up" any, if big sticky tires were used in conjuction with 5,000 rpm launches at the dragstrip etc, etc, etc.<P>well I guess I am through rambling.  In my opinion I would recomend the 3.8 if you want a nice fiero with good power.  Now if you want a striaght line monster that will beat 4th gen f-bodys and hopped up mustangs get the v8.<BR>I went with the 3.8 because it obviously has more power, but most likely be a trouble free car for the next couple of years, till I can get out of school.  <BR>ps- I would really like to hear opinions about drive train reliability with the v8 ||||reg||1
Z||000036||mrfiero||08-18-2000||03:21 AM||||The Getrag 5-sp. trannies are rated at 250 ft. lbs. of torque......the stock V-6 puts out between 160-170, so it has to have a higher rating than 150.<P>Many people have used the Isuzu 5-sp. behind their V-8 without any problems.....it depends on how you drive it.  The Isuzu box can handle it, but the shift forks are the weak link.  The 4-spds were also built by Muncie and are pretty durable (even the 4-cyl ones).  The autos can handle the power of a V-8 without modification, but it's probably a good idea to "beef" it up a bit (hence the JPT auto. trannies that Archie offers).  <P><BR>BTW....there was a 3.3 v-6 offered by GM.  It was a 90* engine based on the 3.8.  It was found in, amongst other models, the '93 Grand Am.   <P>And yes.....the 3.4 that Ed Parks does and talks about is the pushrod version that was found in the '93-'95 Camaro/Firebird.  The 3.4 TDC (found in mid-90s Grand Prixs) is a different animal altogether.  The pushrod 3.4's are essentially a larger 2.8, meaning you can "dress" it up to resemble a stock Fiero V-6 (no one will be able to tell).  With a few minor mods you should approach 200 HP with this engine, but it's still less than a 3.8 or SBC.<P><p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000037||Megafiero||08-18-2000||11:33 AM||||Monkeyman, I agree, I just wanted to help you understand that you can't get a 3.5 or a 3.6 from the 3.4, besides 3.4 does sound better then 3.3 =)<P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.tir.com/~jcabic/megasig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000038||MooCow||08-18-2000||11:55 AM||||WEll if I put the 3.4 pushrod or even the 3.4 DOHC with my 86 4 speed will I be able to have some ample passing power and when I hit the gas will move my little beast.  Right now the V8 and some of 3.8SB sounds hard for me and expensive.  I will haveto see what info I get over the winter and how much money I will get to see what engine I can get.  Can I switch trannies without a problem if I get the tranny from the 3.4 pushrod or the 3.4 DOHC??  Do I have to modify a lot of things to do this or not??<P>                          Thanks Ken||||reg||5
Z||000039||lou_dias||08-18-2000||12:37 PM||||i HAVE ON of Ed's 3.5 swaps in my car with ported heads, intake, Sprint headers, hollow cat, IRM dual exhaust, ADS chip, Accel coil, Bosch Plat 4 way plugs, K&N air filter, Spiralmax intake twister, 1.52 roller rockers, Crane Compucam...did I forget anything?  3.4 injectors, adjustable fuel regulator @ 50 psi...<BR>It's fast...  No body has kept up with me past 110 mph.  I've been up to 148 by my calculations.  And I might turbo it next year and I run 89 octane.<BR>I think next time I will used the Montana mini-van engine and computer and find a turbo for that.  You get more power out of those aluminum heads than our cast iron ones, now that I'm complaining but I don't think the Fiero engine management sensors are up to the task of really making all the power it should have compare to an SFI 3.4 with the same mods.||||reg||1
