Z||000047||Old Lar||08-18-2000||11:48 PM||||I don't know batboy.  My stock 2.8 is running 2400 rpm at 70 in 5th.  IF the car had the horses = 140 mph at 4800 rpm? Red line 6500.  Not enough HP to get to 4000 rpm in 5th gear in my car.  I am more worried about stopping, the Firestones flying apart or some critter jumping out.<P>Man you smack a rabbit at 120 mph, kiss the Fiero nose good by.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000048||mrfiero||08-19-2000||01:29 AM||||If he is running with an Isuzu tranny it is possible to see 140 MPH with a 3.4.  It has a taller final ratio which allows for more top end (assuming there is enough HP to get it there). <P>I believe the Isuzu's have a 3.42 final ratio, while the Getrag a has a 3.65 (not positive on those #'s).<P>||||reg||3
Z||000049||FIEROCIOUS||08-19-2000||02:45 AM||||I have to start the NORTHSTAR propaganda.<P>Northstars need to become as popular as 3.4 swaps! So I can FIND INFO on the internet EASIER!<BR>(ACE if your out there) your prices are rediculous and your willingness to help others is plain shizy!<P>Imagine this if you supply people information and misc. parts. More people would consider Northstar's and then more people would buy kits, wich then people would copy wich would sell more kits and on and on. Keep Northstar convertions for the elite and rich you will not make money!<P>You know guys and gals I realy try to keep e'm short honest!||||reg||1
Z||000050||86SE||08-19-2000||03:20 AM||||I have a final drive of 3.06 on my auto, is that good? or bad?<P>I have $600 put away so far for my 3.4 Firebird pushrod engine.  Should have enough to cover everything by October: Engine from TA Creations for about $400, $200 for shipping from Florida, $? for '88 flexplate, and $200-$400 for other various parts I may need, like: manifold bolts, a lot of other nuts and bolts, and anything else.<P>So I can expect to spend about $750 for a complete swap from a 2.8 to a 3.4 pushrod with about 45,000 miles.  Then I will still have quite a few parts from my 2.8 to sell (only 89,000 miles on the motor).<P>With the Fiero intake system the 3.4 is supposed to have around 180HP and 200lbs torque.  With the exhaust system out of the car and intake system off of the engines I will be able to port it ALL and gain about 30 horses.<P>Possible push about 200bHP?!?!<P>It will make a nice fall Fiero project!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I can't wait til I have about a grand saved up!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||10
Z||000051||olympic||08-19-2000||04:33 AM||||   I think a great combo would be a n/a 3.8 series II with the matching 4 speed auto.  You get 205 hp with a ton of torque and 100% reliability. And all at low(read streetable) rpm, unlike the 3.4 DOHC and the northstar. You'll also probably get 35+ mpg on the freeway. <P>   IF the 205 hp isn't enough you could easily add a 50 shot of N2O and have more torque than you'd know what to do with.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>    ||||reg||1
Z||000052||batboy||08-19-2000||07:50 AM||||I looked up a back issue of my FOCOA magazine, 4th quarter 1998, where they have an article about the fastest Fiero in the world who has an official Bonneville timing slip that documents 147.68 MPH on a Fiero that was specially built to run the salt flats. Lou better get out to Bonneville and break that record so he can get his name in the record books. Come on, let's get real.||||reg||1
Z||000053||Dan86gt3.8||08-19-2000||11:58 AM||||olympic, thats exactly what I have.  I have never actually sat down with the calculator and what not but on the interstate when I am running at 80 I am turning exactly 2,000 rpm.  I think the 35 plus sounds right.  I almost always get 200 miles per tank and I think I got 250 or so out of one tank that was all highway driving.  I never did pay attention to what the stock 2.8 v6 did for gas.  so in general around town the 3.8 is about the same as the stock v6, but on the highway it just hums along sipping the gas.<P>ps- did someone just make an outrageous claim like they invented the question mark?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000054||loafer87gt||08-20-2000||01:46 PM||||Has anyone had the chance to look at one of the Shortstar engines yet? It is a 3.5 litre version of the Caddy Northstar powerplant, and it used in the Intrique, and is supposedly going to be the the replacement engine for the aging 3.8 pushrod powerplant. In my humble opinion, I think that a high revving DOHC engine is much more well suited to the Fiero, how many Ferrari's and Porsche's do you see with pushrod powerplants?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> The numbers for the engine are very impressive, 215 HP, and 230 Torque. It is also all aluminum so it wouldn't upset the weight distribution, if anything it would improve it. Right now one of the biggest problems with a Northstar swap is that the engine is so costly, but with the 3.5 becoming more common, one should be able to find them used or in a salvage yard for a fraction of the cost of a Northstar. ||||reg||1
Z||000055||AkursedX||08-20-2000||03:03 PM||||You know, am I the only here who wants to see how much I can get out of the 2.8??<P>I have a dream of being able to produce maybe 275hp out of the 2.8.  Could it happen??  I think so.  I figure the weakest part would be the crank.  But let's say you get it supercharged, balanced and blueprinted, forged pistons and rods, ported and polished heads and intake, all those bolt on things you can buy with a high flow exhaust, I think you could have one powerful little engine.  Although it would cost more to do this than an engine swap I know, but I would just like to see what someone can get out of the regular 2.8   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000056||lou_dias||08-21-2000||12:34 AM||||The reason I estimate 148 is because I have 225/60/15's on the back and not 215...<BR>I could have gone higher but I was running into traffic.  With practically no exhaust, my engine doesn't mind reving to 5500 before I shift.<BR>  What I am saying is the new FWD 3.4's with SFI, aluminum heads and roller cam produce on average 20hp more hp than the same block with fiero heads and injection setup.  Throw in all my mods and the car would make like 230 hp which puts you lighter than a 3.8 with almost as much power as a 3800SC and you can still add a turbo...<BR>   Does anybody remember the build up article where they claimed GM made 276 gross normally aspirated hp out of this block?  That's about 230 at the wheels right?<BR>One final note... a 60 degree v6 makes more power than a 90 degree v6 with equal displacement.||||reg||1
Z||000057||Formula 18||08-21-2000||12:43 AM||||hey AkursedX<BR>I'd live about an hour away from buffalo and I sure would like to see a 250+HP 2.8L althought I am not sure I'd like to pay for it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Formula 18 (edited 08-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000058||Formula 18||08-21-2000||12:45 AM||||what Al head are you talkingabout Lou??? I thought that thoes head wentto the TDC and weren't interchangable. <P>Secong I've been follow this and I want to know what kinda ofs 1/4 mile time you can expect from these engine with out suping them up past street driveablity and w/o killing your tranny. <BR>What kinda of times can you expect from a 3.4L out of a TA, a ported 3.4L with a little Nitrous (that's probally my choice), a 3800, a s/c 3800 and a SBC. say a 300hp chevy FI 350. ||||reg||1
Z||000059||Black88GT||08-21-2000||12:52 AM||||For what its worth, I would rather build up a 3.4 with all perf. parts (cam, heads, rockers) and use a Miller woods turbo or wait for the new Vortec supercharger.  The swap is about 4 million times easier, a lot cheaper, and same Hp.  I figure $700 for the 3.4, $1,300 to beef it up, and $2,500 for the turbo or SC.  You should run better then a regular old 3.8 SC without the install headaches. Whoever said you can get a SC series II 3.8 for 700 bucks needs to stop smoking rock.  The only thing about the 3.8 is the known reliability that you may not have with the 3.4.||||reg||1
Z||000060||FIEROCIOUS||08-21-2000||12:56 AM||||I've built my fiero 2.8 to the max or close to it for a NA engine.<BR>Cranecam<BR>K&N filter<BR>Roller rockers<BR>Roller chain<BR>bronze guides<BR>Ported and polished heads <BR>Ported lower intake<BR>3.1 fuel injectors<BR>adj. fuel regulator<BR>Stainless steel valves<BR>Headers<BR>300+ Accel ignition<BR>ADS "custom" chip set to current mods<BR>All I have left is extrude honeing the intake manifold!<BR>I have about 190-200 HP on tap. Est.<P>I still can't beat those turbo'd eclipses with the 16g 20psi turbos! Close but not quite! Pisses me off!<P>&lt;ENTER&gt; "Northstar"<P>Bored throttle body<BR>straitpipe<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000061||Wannabe||08-21-2000||01:05 AM||||Teh engine Lou is referring to is known as the 3400. It is from the same v6-60 family as the 2.8, but is about two or three generations newer. It is also offered in the Grand Am GT with 180hp and the Alero with 170 hp. This would be a nice swap because the al heads would save weight, and the new design plus extra displacement really improves torque. This engine, normally aspirated, can put out almost 240 streetable hp, and it shouldn't be too difficult to adapt the Rotrex supercarger kit that is already available for the 2.8 for even more go. Also, with this engine being offered in SO many cars these days, they are sure to be plentiful and cheap in salvage yards. The problem is that the electronics would be just as complicated as a 3.8SC or 3.4 TDC swap.||||reg||7
Z||000062||AkursedX||08-21-2000||11:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula 18:<BR>[B]hey AkursedX<BR>I'd live about an hour away from buffalo and I sure would like to see a 250+HP 2.8L althought I am not sure I'd like to pay for it.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>[B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well as I said in my previous post, It's my "dream" to have a 250 hp 2.8.  Hopefully I will have it one day. (I better start playing the lottery! lol)<P>Fierocious- Sounds like you have a nice setup.  Like you said though, you still only produce around 200hp.  But I think if you had a turbocharger or a supercharger you would be at 250hp, and then you would be able to smooke those talons.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>But keeping my 2.8 is only my personal preference.  I just want to see the max that I can get out of what I was provided.  Don't get me wrong, If I had a chance to do a v-8 or a bigger v-6 swap, of course I would take it!  <P>||||reg||14
Z||000063||lou_dias||08-21-2000||12:24 PM||||Don't forget the stage 2 turbo sets your boost up to 9 psi (from 7) and gives you roughly a 50% boost in power so a 200hp 3.4 would make 300hp...  And be real streetable and maintainable...||||reg||1
Z||000064||BBBAD GT||08-21-2000||02:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B>Don't forget the stage 2 turbo sets your boost up to 9 psi (from 7) and gives you roughly a 50% boost in power so a 200hp 3.4 would make 300hp</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wow, didn't know a 2 psi increase yields 100 hp.  What would 13 psi do?  400 hp?<P>Seriously tho, the standard I've heard is roughly 10-15 rwhp for every pound of boost increase (mainly centrifugal blowers on 5.0 data).  Lots of factors effect it, like if its intercooled, is the fuel system up to it, and basically how efficent your motor is.  Don't get me wrong, forced induction does make power, lots of it!!<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">  <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000065||lou_dias||08-21-2000||10:08 PM||||Well Design One claim 225 hp out of the stock motor that's +85 hp  or over 60% boost in power.  Apply that to a 200hp engine and 300hp sounds tame.||||reg||1
