Z||000070||3400Fiero||09-18-2000||08:28 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MooCow:<BR><B>WEll if I put the 3.4 pushrod or even the 3.4 DOHC with my 86 4 speed will I be able to have some ample passing power and when I hit the gas will move my little beast.  <P>[Oh yeah!  I have a 3400 DOHC in my 88 and love it!  I personally think this is the best conversion because it gives you ample power and doesn't screw up the weight and balance as much as a SBC.]<P>Can I switch trannies without a problem if I get the tranny from the 3.4 pushrod or the 3.4 DOHC??  Do I have to modify a lot of things to do this or not??<BR>                          Thanks Ken</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I bolted my engine up to the stock Fiero tranny using a 88 Fiero flywheel.  Works fine and is no more difficult than the original engine.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000071||StuGood||09-18-2000||03:01 PM||||Forgive me, I'm not smart enough to let this one pass:    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B>... a 60 degree v6 makes more power than a 90 degree v6 with equal displacement.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There's probably a 60-degree V6 somewhere, that makes more power than a 90-degree V6 somewhere.  But I bet it has little or nothing (directly) to do with the angle between the cylinder banks.  <P>If you're thinking of the fact that the 60-degree config. affects the geometry of other parts (e.g. shorter intake runners, etc.), I'll buy it.  All other  things being equal (crankpins spaced for even-firing, equal-length connecting rods, etc.) the crank doesn't really know or care about the orientation of the cylinders around it.<P>Maybe what was meant was, "the 60-degree V6 config. allows longer connecting rods for a given envelope," or something which really does affect output.  Just don't want anyone to come away with the idea that there's something magical about the 60-degree V6 setup. FWIW <P>People get weird ideas about engines.  I remember being asked what the firing order in my 2.8-liter Fiero engine was.  When I said, "1-2-3-4-5-6," they told me I <B>had</B> to be wrong.  "That can't be (snicker)!"<BR>I was smart enough then to just let it go.<BR>            <BR>||||reg||5
